Insertion Sort Code :
import java.util.*;
public class Main{
     public static int[] SelectionSort(int arr[]){
         
         int n = arr.length;
         for(int i = 1; i<=n-1;i++){
             int key = arr[i];
            int j = i-1;
            while(j>=0&&arr[j]>key){
                arr[j+1] = arr[j];
                j--;
            }
            arr[j+1] = key;
             
            System.out.println("Iteration of " + i + ":" +  Arrays.toString(arr));
         }
         
         return arr;
        
     }
    public static void main(String[] args){
        
        int arr[] = {5,4,3,2,1};
        int result[] = SelectionSort(arr);
        
        System.out.print("Final Sorted Array : ");
        for(int x : result){
            System.out.print(x + " ");
        }
        
    }
}

🔎 Step-by-Step Execution (Input: [5,4,3,2,1])
Iteration 1 (i=1, key=4)

Compare 5 > 4 → shift 5.

Insert 4.
Result: [4, 5, 3, 2, 1]

Iteration 2 (i=2, key=3)

Compare 5 > 3 → shift.

Compare 4 > 3 → shift.

Insert 3.
Result: [3, 4, 5, 2, 1]

Iteration 3 (i=3, key=2)

Compare 5 > 2 → shift.

Compare 4 > 2 → shift.

Compare 3 > 2 → shift.

Insert 2.
Result: [2, 3, 4, 5, 1]

Iteration 4 (i=4, key=1)

Compare 5 > 1 → shift.

Compare 4 > 1 → shift.

Compare 3 > 1 → shift.

Compare 2 > 1 → shift.

Insert 1.
Result: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

✅ Final Output:

Iteration 1: [4, 5, 3, 2, 1]
Iteration 2: [3, 4, 5, 2, 1]
Iteration 3: [2, 3, 4, 5, 1]
Iteration 4: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
Final Sorted Array: 1 2 3 4 5

📊 Comparison: Selection vs Bubble vs Insertion
Algorithm	Best Case	Worst Case	Average Case	Stable?	In-place?	When to Use
Selection Sort	O(n²)	O(n²)	O(n²)	❌ No	✅ Yes	When swaps are costly, since it does at most n swaps
Bubble Sort	O(n)	O(n²)	O(n²)	✅ Yes	✅ Yes	When the array is nearly sorted (early stopping helps)
Insertion Sort	O(n)	O(n²)	O(n²)	✅ Yes	✅ Yes	Efficient for small or nearly sorted arrays (like cards sorting)

Key Takeaways

Selection Sort: Always O(n²), but does the minimum number of swaps.

Bubble Sort: Best case O(n) if already sorted (with early stop).

Insertion Sort: Best case O(n), very efficient for nearly sorted data.

All three are O(n²) in worst case, but Insertion Sort is usually fastest in practice for small inputs.


